SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: a rounding whose contact runs off a narrow face onto an earlier
blend of the same radius rolls on that face's far edges, the ball passing
over slivers there (mitcad#1215).

The case: a ring's top, 5 wide between an inner cylinder (radius 88.5)
and an outer rim that an earlier rounding of 2.5 took (its contact on the
top at radius 90.978, 2.478 from the inner rim), with fins at eight mouths
whose crests meet the top there; the inner rim, a closed chain of 17
edges, rounded with 2.5. The ball's contact on the top would lie at radius
91.0, past the top face, and the face beyond its tangent edge is the
earlier blend, curving away from the ball as fast as the ball's own
sphere: the ball touches it along a whole section, not at a point. The
walk overflowed onto it and failed at once. The stored body after the
rounding has the ball passing through the top's far edge all round (the
top face vanishes), its blend a torus through that edge meeting the
earlier blend there. At some mouths that far edge has a sliver edge of 2
to 7 micrometres where a fin's crest meets the top, and at one the inner
cylinder's seam lies under the crest.

1. ChFi3d_Builder::Compute: a stripe whose walk or start failed is computed
   again where a side reaches, within twice the radius, an edge of a face
   of its edges across which the face beyond continues it tangentially and
   curves away from the ball as fast as the ball's sphere or faster (its
   normal curvature across the edge at least 1/(1.01 R),
   RollsBeforeCurvedAway): that side rolls on the edge before that face
   (myRollStripes, as RollsInsteadOfOverflow, and myRollTangentStripes);
   as before where that fails too. The overflow start then rolls there
   although the faces are tangent (StartSolOverflow), and the obstacle
   chaining at a vertex (StartSol) goes on along the next such edge.
2. A sliver edge of the face rolled on between two such edges (shorter
   than 100 tolerances, PassesOverEdge): the ball passes over it. At the
   end of the edge it rolled on it turns about that end until it first
   touches the edge after the sliver (ChFi3d_NextEdges gives that edge,
   BRepBlend_SurfRstLineBuilder::FindTouchOverSliver ends the turn), or it
   rolls on from that edge's start where the ball reaches it already; the
   piece of the ball between (ChFi3d_ConcaveTurnWedge) runs along the
   sliver and the edge after it, with that edge's transition, its curves
   within a periodic face's parameters, so that the face rolled on goes all
   round. A turn forced over a sliver only where the ball leaves the edge.
3. Where the other contact lies within a hair of an edge of its face,
   across which that face goes on tangentially, at the end of the edge
   rolled on (an inner cylinder's seam under a crest): it changes face
   there, the piece before ending on that edge; the ball turns about the
   vertex at the next edge's start rather than the old edge's end (a turn
   of no length there), its other contact a hair outside its face at most.
4. ChFi3d_FilDS: a closed stripe rolling on an edge at its start, where its
   last piece ends on the same edge, cuts that edge with the last piece's
   transition only; a piece whose contact on its first side is a point (the
   ball turning about it) takes the transition of its second side.
Every other fillet is as before.

On item 36's bodies of two versions of the design (the stored body after
the rounding of the outer rim): valid, 343 and 358 faces, the faces of each
body round the rim on the other's within 2 micrometres both ways; each
rounding takes 0.2 s.

